Sending messages:
Programs can send messages to URL(opens in a new tab) addresses such as this: http://127.0.0.1:3000 where HTTP is the protocol(opens in a new tab), 
127.0.0.1 is an IP address, and 3000 is a port(opens in a new tab).
Note the IP part could also be a hostname(opens in a new tab): 127.0.0.1 is also called localhost, so http://localhost:3000 works.

Receiving messages:
Programs can be assigned to listen to any available port. If a program is listening for traffic on port 3000, and a message is sent to that port,
the program will receive and possibly process it.

A message can be sent from the container to localhost; the target is the same container. Similarly, if a request is being sent from outside of a container to localhost,
the target is your machine.
Mapping a port ( port forwarding or port publishing) means connecting a specific port on your host machine to a specific port on a service or container running inside it.
A message can be sent to the container if its listening to that port.
By default, Docker containers run in an isolated network. If a web server runs inside a container listening on port 80,
the computer's browser can't reach it until its mapped to a host port.
* docker run -p 8080:80 nginx # Any network traffic arriving at the computer's port 8080 (Host) is forwarded to port 80 inside the container.
Key Takeaways
-- Isolation Control: Applications running in containers or virtual networks stay hidden and protected until you explicitly open/map a port.
-- Ports Don't Have to Match: Host port 8080 can map to container port 80, host port 2222 can map to SSH port 22, etc.
-- Collision Prevention: two services can't be bound to the exact same host port.
 Port mapping lets you run five different web apps (all using internal port 80) by mapping them to distinct host ports (8001, 8002, 8003, etc.).

Opening a connection from the outside world to a Docker container happens in two steps:
- Exposing port: informing Docker that the container listens to a certain port. This doesn't do much, except it helps humans with the configuration.
Basic syntax: EXPOSE <port> is added in the Dockerfile
- Publishing port: Docker will map host ports to the container ports.
Basic Syntax: docker run -p <host-port>:<container-port> # docker run -p 8080:80 nginx
if the host port is not specified and only the container port is specified, Docker will automatically choose a free port as the host port.
-p           4567	Random free host port → container's 4567
-p           8080:4567	Fixed host port 8080 → container's 4567
-P (capital, no port given)	  Publishes all EXPOSEd ports in the image, each to a random free host port

We could also limit connections to a certain protocol only, e.g. UDP by adding the protocol at the end: EXPOSE <port>/udp and -p <host-port>:<container-port>/udp.

NOTE!!! Security reminder: 
- Opening a port to an application, anyone from the internet could come in and access what is been run.
- Don't haphazardly open just any ports - a way for an attacker to get in is by exploiting any port opened to an insecure server. 
- An easy way to avoid this is by defining the host-side port like this: -p 127.0.0.1:3456:3000.
  # This will only allow requests from your computer through port 3456 to the application port 3000, with no outside access allowed.
  # !!!! The short syntax, -p 3456:3000, will result in the same as -p 0.0.0.0:3456:3000, which truly is opening the port to everyone.!!!!


